\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Ginsparg–Wilson operators and the overlap operator}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Let #{\mathcal H} be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert
space with a self-adjoint \vocabk{grading}{fdip-013N} #{\Gamma},
and let #{a>0}. A complex-linear operator #{K} is
\newvocab{graded-hermitian} if #{K^\dagger=\Gamma K\Gamma}.
A graded-hermitian #{D} is a \newvocab{Ginsparg–Wilson operator}
with spacing #{a} if
##{\Gamma D+D\Gamma=aD\Gamma D.}
This is the relation of
\citet{Section 9.6, Eq. (9.27), p. 24}{wingate2025lattice}.
Its \newvocab{modified grading} is #{\Gamma'=\Gamma(\bbI-aD)}.}

\p{For graded-hermitian #{K} with #{\Gamma K} invertible, let
#{\Gamma_K} act as #{+\bbI} and #{-\bbI} on the positive and
negative eigenspaces, respectively, of the self-adjoint operator
#{\Gamma K}. The \newvocab{overlap operator} of #{K} is
##{D_K=a^{-1}(\bbI+\Gamma\Gamma_K).}
Neuberger likewise adds the unitary polar factor
\citet{Eqs. (8)–(9), (11)–(12), pp. 3–4}{neuberger1998exactly}.}
